 "substitutedBy": null,
 "summary": "Provides science, technology, engineering and mathematics grants for the encouragement of women and minorities to pursue careers in technology.",
 "title": "Provides science, technology, engineering and mathematics (STEM) grants for the encouragement of women and minorities to pursue careers in technology",
 "vetoMessages": {
  "items": [
   {
    "billId": {
     "basePrintNo": "A968",
     "basePrintNoStr": "A968-2015",
     "printNo": "A968",
     "session": 2015,
     "version": ""
    },
    "billPage": 0,
    "chapter": 0,
    "lineEnd": 0,
    "lineStart": 0,
    "memoText": "\n                         VETO MESSAGE - No. 276\n\nTO THE ASSEMBLY:\n\nI am returning herewith, without my approval, the following bill:\n\nAssembly Bill Number 968, entitled:\n\n    \"AN  ACT  to  amend the New York state urban development corporation\n      act, in relation to providing science, technology, engineering and\n      mathematic grants for the encouragement of women and minorities to\n      pursue careers in technology\"\n\n    NOT APPROVED\n\n  This bill would amend the Urban Development Corporation (UDC)  Act  to\nauthorize  the  Empire  State  Development  Corporation  (ESDC) to award\ngrants, on a competitive basis, through the  Economic  Development  Fund\n(EDF)  to  encourage  women  and  minority students to pursue careers in\nscience, technology, engineering, and math  (STEM).  These  STEM  grants\nwould  be  available  to educational institutions supporting grades 6 to\n12, including school districts, charter schools, and boards  of  cooper-\native  education services, to offer programs which promote opportunities\nand careers to women and minority students. Each grant  would  be  on  a\nrecurring  five-year  cycle  with a review completed in concert with the\nState Education Department (SED).\n\n  I fully support STEM education and training as  evidenced  by  several\nstatewide  initiatives  aimed  at  increasing participation of women and\nminorities in STEM fields. For example, the  New  York  State  Board  of\nRegents  has  approved new options for students to meet the State's high\nschool graduation requirements, including pathways in Career and Techni-\ncal Education (CTE) and STEM fields. Further, SED's Office  of  Teaching\nInitiatives  currently offers an expedited pathway to certification as a\nsecondary classroom teacher for college professors  with  advanced  STEM\ndegrees, with the goal to increase STEM offerings in high schools state-\nwide.  In  addition, the New York State Higher Education Services Corpo-\nration operates  the  New  York  State  STEM  Incentives  Program  which\nprovides  a full SUNY or CUNY tuition scholarship to the top ten percent\nof students in each New York State high school if  they  pursue  a  STEM\ndegree  in an associates or bachelor degree program and agree to work in\na STEM field in the State for five years after graduation.\n\n  The bill, however, does not  provide  a  discrete  funding  source  to\nfinance  the  proposed  STEM  grants. If this bill is enacted, the grant\nawards will rely on the same existing funds available in  the  EDF  that\nare  already  committed to other programs. This bill should be discussed\nin the context of the budget. I am therefore constrained  to  veto  this\nbill.\n\n  The bill is disapproved.                    (signed) ANDREW M. CUOMO\n\n                               __________\n",
    "signedDate": null,
    "signer": "ANDREW M. CUOMO",
    "vetoNumber": 276,
    "vetoType": "STANDARD",
